Z||000000||kinboyatuwo||11-13-2000||11:18 AM||||I have had this for quite a while....<BR>when the car is idling the pressure sensor eraticallyu goies from left 0 to top past the top number.  touching the throttle changes and it stabilizes , under load it is stable.  At a cruzing speed it sometimes twitches but not as bad as when it is idling.<P>any help or suggestions<P>I thing the motor is going already it is a 2.5 and has 225000Km on it, hard k's too.  <P>thanks<P>by the way looking to buy a 86-88gt, in or near ontario.  Anyone sees on email me please<P>kinboyatuwo@yahoo.com||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||11-13-2000||11:51 AM||||i'd be willing to bet it's the sending unit is going bad and the plug on the sending unit is brittle and makking a crappy connection. your motor would be hard pressed to make 120+psi oil pressure and them none in a second or 2.. check the sending unit connections before you go diggin really deep in your wallet for a new motor!<P>------------------<BR>"Fondue forks for everybody"||||reg||1
Z||000002||jonny_blaze||11-13-2000||12:31 PM||||hey newbie here, i have the exact same problem.<P>when i start my car the oil pressure is all the way up, after driving around for a while it slowly goes down so far the oil light comes on...im afraid to go far distances because im not sure if oil is circulating, any more input?||||reg||10
Z||000003||jonny_blaze||11-13-2000||01:04 PM||||i will not let this topic die, i need help||||reg||1
Z||000004||Smith 85 2M4||11-13-2000||01:18 PM||||I also think it is the oil pressure sending unit.  I had the same problem with erratic oil pressure on my Fiero and a new sending unit fixed it.  In fact, mine was leaking oil from inside the unit. As for johnny_blaze's problem, I would also look to the oil pressure sending unit.  I highly doubt that the actual oil pressure was enough to max the gauge (unless you are running 20W-50 or some other thick oil).  It is certainly cheaper to try a new sending unit that to tear into the engine.  I think I paid $35 for one at O'Reilly Auto Parts.<P>------------------<BR>Smith 85 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000005||kornholio||11-13-2000||02:48 PM||||Where is the sending unit located on a V6?||||reg||1
Z||000006||286SE'S||11-13-2000||03:08 PM||||Isn't it true that if the oil pressure is actually dropping so low as to cause engine damage that the fuel pump will cut off and the engine will stall. The part of the sender that drives the gauge is not a reliable part.||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||11-13-2000||06:07 PM||||kornholio: Sending unit on V6 is mounted on a bracket, RH forward corner of engine, just to the left of the battery box.  Has a big round 3-terminal connector on top of it - you must squeeze the big ring to get the connector off.  A tube comes up from near the oil filter, and the OP sending unit screws into that, from the top.<P>286SE'S:  Believe it's true the OP sender isn't the most reliable thing ever devised.  I took an old one apart one time - it's probably one of the most Rube Goldberg things I've ever seen.  <P>Lack of oil pressure will <B>not</B> shut the fuel pump off.  The OP fuel pump switch is there to keep you going if the fuel pump relay fails.    <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||DJRice||11-14-2000||11:25 PM||||Actually, I think that there is a safeguard built into the system that shuts the fuel pump down if you loose Oil Pressure.  <P>BTW, I also had erratic oil pressure readings in my 87 v6 and it turned out that my sender was leaking.  I replaced it and no more problems.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||11-15-2000||04:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kornholio:<BR><B>Where is the sending unit located on a V6?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P>its located above the oil filter in the front side of engine under exhaust manifold. could also be a bad or dirty connection at rear of gage pack itself. these are notorious for corrosion. take out the gage cluster and clean brass contacts with a piece of wet or dry sandpaper and reinstall<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Old Lar||11-15-2000||07:34 PM||||I'll try this.  I scanned in an article that SJ Wynman wrote up  in 1997 for the now defunct Tri State Quarterly.  I followed these directions to replace the oil sending unit in my '87.  Its 3 pages long.  I shrunk the pages to 4x5 size before using the PIP  image transfer.  If you can save the pages and blow them up to read the details.  I hope Joe doesn't mind my sharing this repair information.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Page1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Page2.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Page3.jpg"> <P>Thanks Joe.<BR>||||reg||3
